  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agri-enterprise in sunflower growing for women heads of families</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Overall, poverty levels in Uganda are declining but in the North over half the population still lives in poverty. Two decades of civil war and a high prevalence of HIV/AIDS have left women in the region more vulnerable than ever before. After the civil war women returned from camps to head families with on average six dependents including children orphaned by the war. Agriculture is the main source of income for rural households and 90% of employees are women. This project will invest in the cultivation of sunflowers, a commercial crop experiencing increasing demand and prices, which can be grown alongside traditional food crops. This project will support women to access and own land, enhance productivity of crops and link women farmers to more profitable markets. As a result, women and their families will gain increased incomes, which will contributed to better education for orphans, and improved nutrition for all, especially those living with HIV/AIDS.</narrative>
  </description>
